        "body": "Florida's AI enforcement in 2026 runs through child-safety litigation against OpenAI, not through the data center bills that would shape compute supply.\n\nThe four news items are all from September 2026 and all concern the same action: the attorney general seeking to block ChatGPT for minors, to bar OpenAI from developing new models, and to attach criminal penalties to chatbot companies. Three of the four are dated September 28 or 29, so this is one escalation reported several ways rather than a series of separate events. The legislative record is broader but points elsewhere. Of roughly ninety bills, the ones naming artificial intelligence cover agency use, education, therapy and counseling services, companion chatbots, insurance claim denials, and a bill of rights. Data centers appear as their own subject in S0484, H1517, H1007, and a public records exemption in S1118, filed across December 2025 through May 2026.\n\nFor AI scaling, the split matters. The litigation targets model development and consumer access, which is a constraint on deployment. The data center bills sit in the same session but are not connected to the lawsuit in this data, and nothing here reports their contents, their status, or whether they passed. A state that sues a model developer while separately legislating on data center siting is doing two things at once, and the record does not say whether they are coordinated.\n\nWhat this does not cover is large. There are no filing dates for the lawsuit itself, no court, no docket, no outcome, and no response from OpenAI. The bill list gives titles and dates only, so it cannot show what any bill would require or whether it became law. No power, water, land, or utility figures appear anywhere. The reader should treat this as a record of activity, not of effect.\n\nThe four September news items are not four events. Three carry the same date, September 28, and the fourth, September 29, is the injunction request itself, so the record 